ipcd: RTSP sessions cope with RTCP and a full send buffer, and end with the stream

Four old faults in the RTSP server. A client hit by them lost its picture, sometimes
without ever being told.

- RTCP from the client is skipped now. serve_client only knew text requests, so the
  reports stayed in its read buffer. Once that was full, recv with a length of 0
  returned 0, which counted as a closed connection. GStreamer sends a report every
  few seconds and lost the session after about 7.5 minutes, with sound after half of
  that. A text request behind a report ended the session at once as a bad request,
  as the parser stops at the null byte in the frame header. A frame too big for the
  buffer still ends the connection.
- Responses take write_mtx, as the comment at the lock always said. A send sleeping
  on a full buffer lets go of the socket, so a response could land in the middle of
  an RTP packet. ffmpeg then took video for RTSP lines and answered those, the server
  answered that, and on a lasting overload the picture fell to a few frames a second.
- Waiting for that lock must not keep serve_client from a capture restart, as it is
  the thread that wakes a streamer stuck in a send. So sends now wait one second at a
  time, like recv, and look at the restart counter in between. A client still gets 20
  seconds without any progress, what two sends of 10 seconds came to in practice.
- When the streamer gives up, the client learns about it. After "send failed" or "no
  IDR available" the connection stayed open without ever sending a picture again. The
  same now goes for half a response and a sound packet that could not be sent, as the
  stream behind them is broken anyway. A client that leaves or pauses before the first
  key frame is not affected.
- A signal no longer ends a session. On a socket with a timeout, recv and send return
  EINTR even for a signal nobody handles. system() blocks SIGCHLD in its own thread,
  so that the signal goes to another one, as seen on a camera with a small test
  program. ipcd only calls system() while the capture is stopped, so this is a
  precaution. A send that gets interrupted or only gets rid of a part now carries on,
  so that it cannot tear a frame either, which the sound thread and the responses
  could do.

